# Citation metadata — GitHub renders this as "Cite this repository".
#
# The abstract deliberately carries the fidelity statement. Someone who meets
# this work only as a citation, with no access to the repository, still learns
# that the browser twin is a reduced/normalized-unit model and that the paper's
# four predictions are proposed rather than measured. Keep that in any edit;
# it is the same guardrail the README and the paper carry.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Electromagnetic Helix Reactor (EHR): a browser-based reduced-physics plasma simulator"
message: "If you reference this work, please cite it as below."
type: software

authors:
  - given-names: Zachary
    family-names: Auerbach
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-3046-9104"
    affiliation: "Aurelius Dynamic"
    email: build@aureliusdynamic.com

repository-code: "https://github.com/ZachBach/ElectromagneticHelixReactor"
url: "https://github.com/ZachBach/ElectromagneticHelixReactor"
abstract: >-
  A browser-native simulator for a helical-antenna plasma reactor, built as the
  companion digital twin to the accompanying paper. Roughly 18,000
  macro-electrons are advanced through an analytic magnetic field with a Boris
  integrator and Monte-Carlo collisions against a neutral background, rendered
  live in 3D alongside six synthetic diagnostics — Langmuir probes, a turbulence
  proxy, an eight-probe B-dot array recovering the azimuthal mode by DFT, a
  synthetic GPI camera, axial structure under acoustic drive, and a dusty-plasma
  panel with pair correlation. Three coupling models (KERNEL, EMERGENT, WAVE)
  let a hand-tuned prior be compared against coupling derived from geometry
  alone. A separate headless SI-units transport code under sim/ measures
  confinement time against field-line pitch with real argon cross sections and
  absorbing walls.

  Principal result of the simulation programme: self-consistent electric fields
  reduce the pitch dependence of bulk electron confinement from the
  approximately 2.8x that test-particle transport predicts to at most 1.1x in
  sustained steady state, on both sides of the ion-magnetization boundary
  (exit-speed ion Hall parameter H_i* up to 2.35). Sweeping across that boundary did not
  restore the advantage; the gain declined monotonically to 0.97. The strong
  form of the regime-gated confinement hypothesis is therefore NOT SUPPORTED in
  this model system. Field-line pitch instead controls the distribution of
  transport — which loss channel and which species carries the flux, and where
  the trapped density sits — making it a profile-control parameter rather than
  a confinement-control parameter. A secondary correction: the argon crossing
  of exit-speed ion Hall parameter H_i* = 1 sits near 3.6 kG at 10 mTorr, not the 530 G
  where the thermal ion Hall parameter reaches 1.

  Fidelity, stated as part of the record: the browser model is REDUCED and runs
  in NORMALIZED UNITS on analytic fields, macro-particles and a coarse
  64x64x128 grid — trends and comparisons between settings are meaningful,
  absolute values are not. The headline verdict above comes from the separate
  headless SI-units code and covers the TRANSPORT PATHWAY ONLY; the model
  contains no RF heating, so pitch-dependent power deposition remains untested.
  The concept paper's four experimental predictions are FALSIFIABLE PROPOSALS,
  NOT MEASUREMENTS; no physical reactor has been built and NO LABORATORY DATA
  is reported here — every number is simulated. The word "digital twin" is used
  in the modelling sense, not the telemetry-backed sense: nothing in this
  repository mirrors an instrumented physical device. All simulation runs are
  seeded and reproducible.

keywords:
  - plasma physics
  - helicon
  - dusty plasma
  - Boris integrator
  - Monte-Carlo collisions
  - particle-in-cell
  - scientific visualization
  - three.js
  - simulation

